  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Chilly weather persists through Sunday morning.\n\nA surface high pressure ridge crests E'ward across western and \ncentral NY tonight while the ridge aloft builds from the Upper \nMidwest and vicinity. Associated dry air and subsidence will \nsupport dry weather and primarily clear sky across western and \ncentral New York as we remain in the cold sector behind \nyesterday's cold front passage. However, a sufficiently-cold\nlow-level air mass, WSW'erly to SW'erly mean flow in the \nsurface to 925 mb layer on the backside of the surface ridge, \nand the resulting increase in fetch and mesoscale low-level \nmoisture, respectively, are expected to yield meager lake-induced\ninstability and allow shallow and isolated to scattered lake-effect\nstratocumuli to develop and stream generally ENE'ward and then \nNE'ward from the ~16C to ~18C eastern Great Lakes late this \nevening through daybreak Sunday morning. The overall lack of \ncloud cover will certainly support radiational cooling through \nshortly after daybreak, but with the surface high centered south\nof our region, a light breeze should persist in most areas and \nlimit radiational cooling to some extent. A Freeze Warning \nremains in effect for areas where the growing season is still \nactive, but the prospect of a light breeze persisting lowers \nconfidence that temps will drop below 32F in some areas.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An extended period of dry and warmer weather begins \nSunday afternoon and likely lasts through next weekend.\n\nThe surface ridge axis drifts E'ward toward the coast of the \nNew England and Mid-Atlantic states on Sunday as the ridge aloft\ncrests E'ward over our region. Continued SW'erly low-level flow\non the backside of the surface ridge will result in WAA, which\nwill cause any lingering lake-effect stratocumuli over and \ndownwind of the eastern Great Lakes to dissipate by midday as \nmeager lake-induced instability wanes. Highs will reach the \nlower 60s for lower elevations and mid 50s for higher terrain \nand the North Country after a chilly start.\n\nSunday will begin an extended stretch of dry and sunny weather that \nwill likely last all the way through next weekend. High pressure \nsurface and aloft will take up residence across much of the central \nand eastern CONUS, with the pattern stagnating for an extended \nperiod of time. This will yield dry weather and plenty of sunshine \neach day for the foreseeable future. A cutoff low will be trapped \nwithin the ridge just off the Carolina and Mid Atlantic coast all \nweek, but model and ensemble consensus keeps this system well \nremoved from the eastern Great Lakes.",
